# Prelude No. 1 in C major, Well-Tempered Clavier I, BWV 846 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: one broken-chord figure, the same in every bar, through a
# slow-moving chord progression. There is no melody to bring out: the
# music is the harmony, one chord per bar, so the shaping follows the
# tension and release of the chords, quiet and unhurried.
#
# Time signature and bars: 4/4, 35 bars, as in the Bach-Gesellschaft
# text and this Mutopia edition (no Schwencke bar between 22 and 23).
# Bar numbers match the score and the MIDI.
#
# Tempo: quarter = 64. 35 bars is 140 quarters (the last bar held). From
# catalogue listings: Michael Houstoun 2:17 (about 61), Robert Silverman
# 2:27 (about 57), Daniel Barenboim (2006) 1:40 (about 84). Two listings
# credited to Glenn Gould give 2:22 and 2:24 (about 58), but I could not
# confirm which Gould recording they are, and I found no confirmed
# per-prelude timings for Schiff, Hewitt, Richter, Tureck, Gulda,
# Leonhardt or Gilbert. 64 sits a little above the measured piano middle
# (57 to 61): the chip plucks like a harpsichord, and the figure should
# flow, not drag.
#
# Structure (harmony, bar by bar):
#   1-4    C: I, ii 4/2, V 6/5, I. The tonic stated.
#   5-11   outward to G: A minor (5), D7 over C (6), G (7), and a
#          cadence in G major at 11.
#   12-19  back home by a descending, chromatic bass: diminished chords
#          (12, 14), D minor, F, to the cadence in C at 19.
#   20-23  C7 (20) opens a last push: F major 7 (21), then diminished
#          sevenths on F sharp (22) and G sharp (23), the darkest point.
#   24-31  the dominant pedal on G: G7, 6/4 chords, a diminished seventh
#          over the pedal at 28, rising tension held over the low G.
#   32-35  the tonic pedal on C: C7 (32), the figure changes (33-34) and
#          the final C major chord (35).
#
# Voicing: every note of the figure starts as the highest sounding pitch
# (the held bass and tenor start first, then each sixteenth lies above
# them), so the converter ranks almost every note "top" and voice
# balance cannot separate the lines here. The terraces below do the work,
# with a light accent on each bar's first note, the bass, which carries
# the change of harmony.
#
# Range: the chip's lowest note is about B2, so the low bass of bars 18
# and 21-35 (G2, F2, F sharp 2, G sharp 2, C2) is lifted an octave. The
# dominant and tonic pedals therefore sound at G3 and C3, inside the
# texture rather than under it; the terraces have to carry the weight
# the low pedal would give on a keyboard.

tempo 64

accent 1          # the bass on each downbeat: the new chord, felt

dyn 1 mp          # bars 1-4: the tonic stated, quietly
dyn 5 mf          # bars 5-11: opening out toward G
dyn 12 mp         # bars 12-19: the chromatic way back to C, darker
dyn 20 mf         # bars 20-23: C7 and the diminished chords, tension
ramp 24-31 f      # bars 24-31: the dominant pedal, the one long build
dyn 32 mp         # bars 32-35: the tonic pedal, release

broaden 11 0.95   # into the cadence in G
broaden 19 0.95   # into the cadence in C

rit 3 0.7         # bars 33-35: the closing figure and the final chord
